Lunar elevation data
--------------------
data/terrain/ holds heights cut and resampled from NASA's lunar elevation
maps by tools/terrain/build_terrain.py:

- NAC_DTM_APOLLO11, NAC_DTM_APOLLO12, NAC_DTM_APOLLO14,
  NAC_DTM_APOLLO15 and NAC_DTM_APOLLO16, the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter
  Camera's 2 m digital terrain models of the Apollo 11, 12, 14, 15 and 16
  landing sites (Henriksen et al., 2017). Credit: NASA/GSFC/Arizona State University. PDS volume
  LROLRC_2001.
- SLDEM2015, the Lunar Orbiter Laser Altimeter merged with SELENE Terrain
  Camera stereo, 512 px/deg (Barker et al., 2016, Icarus 273). Credit:
  NASA/GSFC; JAXA/SELENE for the Terrain Camera data. PDS volume
  LRO-L-LOLA-4-GDR-V1.0.
- LDEM_4, the Lunar Orbiter Laser Altimeter's 4 px/deg global map.
  Credit: NASA/GSFC. PDS volume LRO-L-LOLA-4-GDR-V1.0.

These are data, not this project's code: use them with the credits
above.

Lunar and solar ephemeris
-------------------------
data/ephemeris/ holds Chebyshev series fitted by
tools/ephemeris/fit_ephemeris.py, one for each mission's dates, and
tests/fixtures/ephemeris_check*.json the values held out of each fit:

- The Moon's and the Sun's geocentric positions: JPL's DE441 planetary
  and lunar ephemeris (Park et al., 2021, Astron. J. 161:105), from the
  JPL Horizons system (https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/). Credit:
  NASA/JPL-Caltech Solar System Dynamics Group.
- The Moon's orientation: the IAU Working Group on Cartographic
  Coordinates and Rotational Elements' 2009 model (Archinal et al., 2011,
  Celest. Mech. Dyn. Astr. 109:101), as NAIF's text kernel pck00010.tpc
  gives it (https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/). Credit: NASA/JPL Navigation and
  Ancillary Information Facility.
- TAI - UTC for 1968 to 1971 from the US Naval Observatory's
  tai-utc.dat.

Navigation stars
----------------
data/stars/navigation_stars.json holds the positions of the Apollo
guidance computers' 37 navigation stars, taken by
tools/stars/navigation_stars.py from the Hipparcos catalogue's new
reduction (van Leeuwen, 2007, Astron. Astrophys. 474:653; CDS catalogue
I/311) through the VizieR service (Ochsenbein et al., 2000, Astron.
Astrophys. Suppl. 143:23), and carried by their proper motions to
Apollo 11's launch day. Credit: ESA, the Hipparcos mission; CDS,
Strasbourg.

